        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): use Object.hasOwn instead of in operator in printSessionUpdate",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe `printSessionUpdate` helper in `src/acp/client.ts:75` uses the `in` operator to check for `sessionUpdate` on the notification update object. The `in` operator traverses the prototype chain, so it would match inherited properties — though unlikely in practice, `Object.hasOwn()` is the more correct semantic for own-property checks.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`src/acp/client.ts`** — replace `!(\"sessionUpdate\" in update)` with `!Object.hasOwn(update, \"sessionUpdate\")` (1 line)\n\n## Evidence\n\nDirect behavior probe — `Object.hasOwn` vs `in` operator equivalence for own-property checks:\n\n```text\n$ node -e \"...\"\n=== Object.hasOwn vs in operator equivalence ===\nOwn property - Object.hasOwn: true\nOwn property - in operator: true\nMissing property - Object.hasOwn: false\nMissing property - in operator: false\nInherited only - Object.hasOwn: false\nInherited only - in operator: true\n\nThe ACP update object has sessionUpdate as own property,\nso Object.hasOwn and in operator are equivalent here.\nObject.hasOwn is safer: it would NOT match a prototype-injected property.\n```\n\nThe ACP SDK contract requires and validates the `sessionUpdate` discriminator as an own property. This change is purely mechanical and behavior-preserving.\n\n## Related\n\nN/A (self-contained code hygiene improvement)\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
        "branch": "fix-acp-object-hasown",
